tion shapes is a strong indicator the network is numerically tame under FP16 for linear layers.\n  - Mitigation: keep SDPA and softmax in BF16 (as you already are). That’s where range really matters.\n\n- Medium risk: rare overflow/underflow sites\n  - SwiGLU: A = xW_a, B = xW_b; swish(A)·B. The swish nonlinearity plus gating can amplify tails. Watch the distribution of A and B pre- and post-norm. FP16 overflow at |x| > 65504; underflow below ~6e‑8.\n  - AdaLN modulation: per-channel scale/shift can push activations into less friendly ranges for the following linear. Track |scale| stats.\n  - Residual adds in BF16: quantization-plus-residual feedback can drift if a block’s linear outputs get a tad noisier. Monitor residual norm ratio.\n\n- Low risk: matmul K-dimension reduction error\n  - With HEAD_DIM=128 and FFN=14336, FP16 roundoff across K accumulates to O(√K·ulp). Your 2e‑4 relative error is in line with expectation for FP16 acc on normalized inputs.\n\n- What to A/B (fast to run, high sensitivity)\n  - Layerwise probes on a fixed held-out batch (10–20 prompts, 2 seeds each):\n    - For each DiT block: cosine similarity of hidden state before and after the block vs BF16 baseline; threshold >0.999 across all layers; flag any layer <0.997.\n    - RMS of block outputs; 5th/95th percentile; max |x|; NaN/Inf counts must be zero.\n    - Attention: entropy distribution per head; KL divergence vs baseline <1e‑3 mean, <5e‑3 max.\n  - End-to-end image/video metrics over your gates:\n    - Identity IoU, seam/temporal-consistency gate, body-plan gate: no degradation beyond your current seed-pinned variance. Also compute LPIPS/SSIM/PSNR deltas; require median LPIPS delta < 0.005 and 95th percentile < 0.015.\n    - Predicted-noise SNR vs baseline for each step: correlation r^2 > 0.999; per-pixel mean abs diff normalized by sigma_t < 0.3%.\n  - Stress cases:\n    - Max-length text + high-contrast frames + loud audio segments.\n    - Extreme seeds that previously produced high logits/activations (logits norm p95).\n  - If any fail, fallback to pre-transposed BF16 (your 111 s number).\n\n2) SDPA optimization options after GEMM fix (ranked, with expected impact)\n- 1) Custom FlashAttention-style kernel (plain SIMD, online softmax, no simdgroup_matrix)\n  - Expected GPU gain: 2–3× on Navi21 (RX 6900 XT) because you eliminate the 5.9 GiB scores tensor and turn two large matmuls + softmax into IO-efficient tiles in LDS with streaming max/exp/sum. Your current 65 s GPU should drop to ~22–33 s. CPU encode overhead (~47 s) also drops dramatically since you’ll submit a couple of compute passes per block rather than an MPSGraph SDPA op.\n  - Feasibility: RDNA2 has ample LDS (64 KiB/CU) and fast scalar exp/log approximations; you can implement block sizes like (Br,Bc)=(128,64) with head-splitting intact and numerically stable online softmax (max-subtraction + running sums in fp32 registers). Even without tensor ops, this is worth it.\n  - Numerics: keep logits accumulation and softmax stats in FP32; inputs/outputs BF16. This matches common FA practice and is safer than FP16 SDPA.\n\n- 2) Leave SDPA in MPSGraph BF16 but attack encode overhead and dispatch granularity\n  - Fold QK^T, softmax, and P·V for all heads of a block into a single reusable MPSGraphExecutable; reduce per-dispatch CPU time by batching heads or sequences so you issue 50 SDPA dispatches per generation instead of 200. Expected CPU encode savings: 2–3× (47 s → 15–25 s). GPU time will barely change (still memory/IO bound).\n  - Alternatively, reimplement SDPA with MPSMatrixMultiplication + custom softmax in between (two matmuls + one kernel). You’ll likely land within ±10% of current GPU time but cut encode cost to a few seconds total.\n\n- 3) FP16 SDPA in MPSGraph\n  - Expected benefit: minimal to none on GPU time (both BF16 and FP16 are 2-byte; SDPA is bandwidth/IO dominated). Might shave a few percent if math pipes saturate on qk^T/PV. Risk: if MPSGraph keeps softmax in 16-bit, you can induce underflow in the softmax tails, causing attention dropout and quality regressions. Rank this last.\n\n3) Pipelining depth vs macOS eGPU watchdog; MPSGraph interleaving footguns (ranked)\n- 1) Two in-flight command buffers, each <0.5 s GPU, is safe\n  - macOS watchdog on external AMD generally trips on single CBs >~2–5 s or when the WindowServer misses several frames. Your 0.3–0.5 s CBs with ≤2 in flight are well under risk thresholds.\n\n- 2) Avoid sharing one MPSGraphExecutable concurrently across two streams unless documented thread-safe for concurrent encode\n  - In practice, parallel encodes against the same Executable can serialize internally or create hidden fences. Safer: instantiate two Executables (or two Graphs) bound to the same device; one per stream. Reuse them across the whole run.\n\n- 3) Use MTLSharedEvent/MTLFence for slot protection; never CPU wait\n  - Signal a SharedEvent when a block’s last kernel (or blit) finishes; have the weight-upload stream wait on that event before reusing the slot. Don’t call waitUntilCompleted; rely on addCompletedHandler for CPU-side lifecycle.\n\n- 4) Overlap blit (TB3 uploads) with compute\n  - RX 6900 XT exposes SDMA engines; Metal will overlap blit and compute when in separate CBs. Expect good overlap as long as your copy sizes are large (tens to hundreds of MiB) and you don’t flood the queue with many tiny CBs. Keep the in-flight limit at 2; larger can backpressure the TB3 path and increase jitter.\n\n- 5) Don’t use commitAndContinue across independent streams\n  - commitAndContinue chains encoders within one CB; it doesn’t help inter-stream overlap and can accidentally serialize. Prefer one CB per block per stream, explicit events, and short CBs.\n\n4) Zero-output-change optimizations you can still do (ranked, cheapest first)\n- 1) Switch GEMMs to MPSMatrixMultiplication FP16/FP32 directly for encode-time savings\n  - Your table shows MPSMM FP16 performance equals MPSGraph FP16 (≈27–28 TFLOP/s), but encode overhead with MPSMM is tiny. This alone can claw back tens of seconds of CPU gaps (you reported 0.2–0.9 s CPU encode per block today).\n\n- 2) Pre-transpose weights offline and store [K,N] on disk\n  - Eliminates in-graph or post-upload transposes without any numerical change. Even if you later must fall back to BF16, the “pre-transposed BF16” path is 5× faster (580 s → 111 s for GEMMs).\n\n- 3) Use MTLHeaps and persistent private buffers for weight slots and activations\n  - Pre-allocate two or three large heaps (weights A/B/C, activations workspace). Keeps resource residency stable, reduces driver bookkeeping, and improves overlap of blits + compute. Mark transient scratch as hazardTrackingModeUntracked when you fully manage hazards.\n\n- 4) Batch/fuse per-block ops to reduce dispatch count\n  - Encode each block as a single CB containing: norm/affine → QKV GEMM → SDPA (if you stay MPSGraph) → out GEMM → MLP GEMMs + elementwise. Fewer CBs → fewer TB3 round-trips → smaller CPU encode gaps. Keep CB <0.5 s.\n\n- 5) Warm up and reuse all pipelines (MTLBinaryArchive)\n  - Prebuild all custom Metal pipelines and keep them alive. For MPSGraph, build Executables once and only feed new tensors. This ensures encode is pure command recording, not compilation.\n\n- 6) Upload via blit from pinned staging buffers\n  - Stage into shared/page-aligned/upload-optimized buffers and blit to private buffers. Large, aligned copies give better TB3 throughput and better overlap than newBufferWithBytes for every chunk.\n\n- 7) Weight-slot packing\n  - For each block, co-pack qkv/out/fc1/fc2 into one contiguous device buffer per slot to reduce the number of blit commands and resource bindings per block.\n\n5) Can you avoid re-uploading 38.5 GiB/step over TB3 on 16 GiB VRAM without quality loss?\n- Fully avoid: no. You can’t fit 38.5 GiB of weights plus activations and SDPA workspaces into 16 GiB.\n- Partially avoid (recommended):\n  - Pin a subset of blocks across all steps. Each block ≈0.77 GiB (qkv ≈0.215 GiB, out ≈0.077 GiB, fc1 ≈0.287 GiB, fc2 ≈0.154 GiB; sum ≈0.73–0.78 GiB/block). Budgeting ~8–10 GiB VRAM for weights leaves room to pin 10–12 blocks resident across the 4 steps. That saves ~7.7–9.2 GiB per step (≈31–37 GiB over 4 steps), cutting TB time by ~14–17 s warm.\n  - Prioritize the heaviest blocks (fc1/fc2 dominate) or blocks with worst blit overlap.\n\n- Compression/quantization over TB3:\n  - Lossless compression: BF16 weights are effectively random; expect <1.1× compression. Not worth it.\n  - INT8 weights with on-GPU dequantization: would cut TB bytes in half but is lossy and requires INT8 matmul or dequantize-then-FP16 GEMM. MPSGraph INT8 matmul on AMD eGPU is not optimized (no tensor ops/DPAS); you’ll fall back to scalarized integer MACs and lose performance. Not recommended for this card.\n  - FP8/INT4 style schemes: also lossy; you asked “without quality loss,” so out of scope.\n\n- Host-mapped zero-copy: not available for AMD eGPU on macOS in a way that helps; you still traverse TB3 and pay the bandwidth.\n\nBottom-line, your plan and estimates look solid:\n- Software pipelining should erase the 178 s wall-vs-GPU gap you observed at 22f.\n- FP16 GEMM is the right lever on this hardware; expect GEMM ≈35–45 s/generation and total denoise ≈130–150 s once overlapped.\n- SDPA becomes the next bottleneck; a plain-SIMD FlashAttention kernel is worth building and should drop SDPA GPU to ~22–33 s and nuke most of the 47 s CPU encode.\n- Keep 10–12 blocks resident across steps to shave ~15 s of TB per 4-step run.\n- If any FP16 A/B fails, flip to pre-transposed BF16 (≈111 s GEMM) while you pursue FlashAttention to keep denoise ≲200 s.","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Evaluating range risk**\n\nI'm considering the risk related to range normalization, specifically using q and k normalized by 1/sqrt(d) which comes out to about 0.088. When dealing with the dot products in 128 dimensions, the typical logits might range from -20 to 20, which should be fine for fp16, but there’s a risk of exponent underflow for larger negatives. Softmax might underflow to zero, creating attention dropout, though stable softmax handles it better. \n\nI also have various options to consider, like optimizing CPU encoding overhead and utilizing MPSGraph more efficiently.**Assessing performance optimization**\n\nI'm exploring ways to reduce encoding overhead in MPSGraph. One option is using MPSGraphScheduleWithDevice or manually employing MPSMatrixMultiplication for SDPA. Another interesting idea is implementing a custom FlashAttention-like kernel using SIMD, which could provide significant speed improvements, about 2-3 times faster than MPSGraph thanks to reduced memory bandwidth. \n\nI've calculated that current GPU dispatches take 65 seconds for 200 runs, translating to 0.325 seconds each. With FlashAttention, we might get that down to 22-33 seconds. There’s quite a bit of implementation effort, but it seems doable!**Analyzing encoding efficiency**\n\nI'm looking into how using a custom MTLComputePipeline with a fixed grid can drastically reduce CPU encoding times, possibly down to just a few milliseconds. I thought FP16 SDPA might help with compute efficiency by about 1.3x, but since both BF16 and FP16 use 2 bytes, there's no real memory saving. The main cost seems to come from memory bandwidth related to large scores. The attention calculations, particularly the GEMMs, don’t show a huge difference between FP16 and BF16, so it seems FP16 SDPA may not be as promising as I hoped.**Focusing on custom kernel optimization**\n\nI'm shifting my focus to the custom kernel. Regarding Question 3, it's essential to consider the balance between pipelining depth and the watchdog timer. They suggest keeping the command buffers (CBs) inflight to a maximum of 2 for around 0.3-0.5 seconds after a fix. The macOS watchdog for eGPU scenarios can be quite variable, sometimes allowing up to 10 seconds. \n\nWith the AMD macOS Metal driver, there's a single hardware compute queue, limiting overlapping blit and compute operations. Using separate MTLCommandBuffers and MTLSharedEvents for hazards seems like a wise strategy!**Probing MPSGraph for int8 matmul**\n\nI'm considering the feasibility of using MPSGraph for int8 matrix multiplication on the gfx1030, but it seems that support for quantized operations is primarily aimed at the iOS Neural Engine. Performance on AMD eGPU might not be optimal, possibly leading to poor results. While there's MPSMatrixMultiplication for integer operations, the overall value appears limited given the low performance expected for int8 dot products.\n\nI also want to explore methods to manage weight residency effectively, perhaps by keeping a selection of weights in memory across steps to minimize bandwidth usage. This could potentially help lower data transfer around the Thunderbolt 3 bottleneck, but limitations like the need for decompression and the size of weights still pose challenges. Maintaining a \"hot subset\" of blocks could help with efficiency!**Evaluating MPSGraph for matmul**\n\nI’ve concluded that AMD's half-precision matmul kernels in MPSGraph don’t allow for FP32 accumulation unless specifically set, leaving a risk in terms of numerical precision when using FP16. I need to validate this further, perhaps by measuring specific performance metrics, checking for errors, and monitoring various outputs.\n\nFor data management, strategies are key, like using separate command buffers and efficient weight storage. I’ll suggest improvements like utilizing MPSMatrixMultiplication to minimize encoding time significantly while providing a detailed ranking of options for performance.\n\nLastly, using BF16 for transposed weights and managing memory effectively will be crucial!**Considering weight management strategies**\n\nI think the best approach is to pin 8-12 blocks across processing steps, which could save about 6-10 GiB per step. However, using GPU local memory for weight paging with compressed formats doesn't seem feasible. \n\nFor MPSGraph's int8 matmul on AMD, I’m concerned it might be slow and scalarized, so it’s not worth pursuing. I could suggest 12-bit per-channel quantization with on-the-fly dequantization to FP16 during matmul—though this could impact quality. \n\nFinally, I’ll include clear bullet points with rankings for each question and emphasize the importance of testing these approaches!","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.summary","summary":"**Evaluating range risk**\n\nI'm considering the risk related to range normalization, specifically using q and k normalized by 1/sqrt(d) which comes out to about 0.088. When dealing with the dot products in 128 dimensions, the typical logits might range from -20 to 20, which should be fine for fp16, but there’s a risk of exponent underflow for larger negatives. Softmax might underflow to zero, creating attention dropout, though stable softmax handles it better. \n\nI also have various options to consider, like optimizing CPU encoding overhead and utilizing MPSGraph more efficiently.**Assessing performance optimization**\n\nI'm exploring ways to reduce encoding overhead in MPSGraph. One option is using MPSGraphScheduleWithDevice or manually employing MPSMatrixMultiplication for SDPA. Another interesting idea is implementing a custom FlashAttention-like kernel using SIMD, which could provide significant speed improvements, about 2-3 times faster than MPSGraph thanks to reduced memory bandwidth. \n\nI've calculated that current GPU dispatches take 65 seconds for 200 runs, translating to 0.325 seconds each. With FlashAttention, we might get that down to 22-33 seconds. There’s quite a bit of implementation effort, but it seems doable!**Analyzing encoding efficiency**\n\nI'm looking into how using a custom MTLComputePipeline with a fixed grid can drastically reduce CPU encoding times, possibly down to just a few milliseconds. I thought FP16 SDPA might help with compute efficiency by about 1.3x, but since both BF16 and FP16 use 2 bytes, there's no real memory saving. The main cost seems to come from memory bandwidth related to large scores. The attention calculations, particularly the GEMMs, don’t show a huge difference between FP16 and BF16, so it seems FP16 SDPA may not be as promising as I hoped.**Focusing on custom kernel optimization**\n\nI'm shifting my focus to the custom kernel. Regarding Question 3, it's essential to consider the balance between pipelining depth and the watchdog timer. They suggest keeping the command buffers (CBs) inflight to a maximum of 2 for around 0.3-0.5 seconds after a fix. The macOS watchdog for eGPU scenarios can be quite variable, sometimes allowing up to 10 seconds. \n\nWith the AMD macOS Metal driver, there's a single hardware compute queue, limiting overlapping blit and compute operations. Using separate MTLCommandBuffers and MTLSharedEvents for hazards seems like a wise strategy!**Probing MPSGraph for int8 matmul**\n\nI'm considering the feasibility of using MPSGraph for int8 matrix multiplication on the gfx1030, but it seems that support for quantized operations is primarily aimed at the iOS Neural Engine. Performance on AMD eGPU might not be optimal, possibly leading to poor results. While there's MPSMatrixMultiplication for integer operations, the overall value appears limited given the low performance expected for int8 dot products.\n\nI also want to explore methods to manage weight residency effectively, perhaps by keeping a selection of weights in memory across steps to minimize bandwidth usage. This could potentially help lower data transfer around the Thunderbolt 3 bottleneck, but limitations like the need for decompression and the size of weights still pose challenges. Maintaining a \"hot subset\" of blocks could help with efficiency!**Evaluating MPSGraph for matmul**\n\nI’ve concluded that AMD's half-precision matmul kernels in MPSGraph don’t allow for FP32 accumulation unless specificall